  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Greenpeace Greece LAC Ecofeminist camp</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In just two years, the Climate Justice Camp (CJC) has become the largest global platform of its kind, bringing together hundreds oforganizers, activists, advocates, and community leaders working at the intersection of climate and social justice each year. Following on from the success of the camp initiative, in early 2025 we will be hosting a gathering specifically for ecofeminist organizersand activists from Latin America. An extension of the Climate Justice Camp, the Ecofeminist Camp will be centeredin developing knowledge, strategies, capacity, and networks among communities on the front-lines of the climate crisis, to support real-world and long-term action.

Across a week, participants and partners will develop, engage in, and lead workshops andeventsfocused on a series of thematic tracks with concrete objectives. These will be based in the centralecofeminist paradigm, &quot;the sustainability of life&quot;, addressing extractivism and the impacts of theclimate crisis from a feminist perspective in LatinAmerica, with the aim of amplifying andstrengthening a more robust, diverse, and resilient climate movement.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Greenpeace Greece</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Greenpeace exists because this planet needs a voice. It needs solutions. Change is needed. Action is needed.

The establishment of the Greek office in 1991 came with an impressive action on the Isthmus of Corinth with the message &quot;No more oil in the sea&quot;. In the following years, we stopped the construction of waste incineration plants, cancelled the expansion of PETROLA&apos;s refineries. For climate and energy, we are fighting to wean our country off fossil fuels and exploit our inexhaustible natural resources, such as the sun. We defend good, healthy food and support farmers who grow crops following the principles of sustainable agriculture. To protect the sea, we&apos;re giving a voice to low-intensity coastal fishermen, creating a fair fish market that brings them closer to consumers, and showing the solution against single-use plastic. But above all, through our work we want to inspire peaceful collective action and give real power to civil society by doing what no one else can do. Each of us can play a catalytic role for the common good, as long as we believe in ourselves and our abilities.

That&apos;s why we work side by side with people, initiatives, groups and organizationsand exchange knowledge and experiences (such as peaceful dynamic action, analysis and strategic approach). We put new ideas into practice by example and reinforce stories of hope inside and outside our network to convey courage and optimism.</narrative>
  </participating-org>
